The trajectory no longer says "these are live" when the CI listing it reads stopped at the newest failure. A real live failure still says it, word for word. Committed as 6c64ebbd, and only scripts/extract_trajectory.py changed.

Cause: CI has been green for a week, but tonight's trajectory said "these are live". The gh listing it reads came back a week out of date, and its newest row was the failure itself. page_is_stale treats a tie between those two times as "fresh", so the old page was taken as proof CI was red.

Fix: a tie isn't treated as an answer either way, because a genuinely live failure looks the same. Instead, on a tie the script asks gh one more time, without the --status completed filter the old page came through, and decides from that:
• **The second listing shows something newer:** "gone green" if it's a success, otherwise "could not determine". That message now says the listing "stopped exactly at" the failure instead of "predates" it.
• **It agrees the failure is the newest run:** still-live, same sentence as before.
• **It fails, comes back empty, or was never run:** "could not check". Never "live", never "green".

The decision logic has no gh calls in it, so the self-tests can drive it directly. The extra call only happens on a tie. The stderr receipt line now also records tie_check= and tie_newest_completed=, so the next false alarm can be diagnosed from the log.

Tests: the fixtures are tonight's actual receipt and the second listing as I captured it by hand around 21:00. They cover:
• the real tie, which now reads gone-green;
• a real live failure, which still prints the exact same sentence;
• six ways the second listing can fail;
• the receipt fields;
• the cases that weren't ties, which haven't changed.

To check the tests can fail, I made the tie branch ignore the second listing (changed and restored in a single command). 11 tests failed by name, the real-tie case first. After restoring, all 493 self-tests, tests/harness_logic.sh (161), clippy, fmt and cargo test pass.

The new path hasn't run for real yet. When I ran the script against GitHub tonight the listing was up to date, so there was no tie (tie_check=not-asked), and nothing else in the loop parses the receipt line. The commit message says this. The function docstring now lists this as one more case where a cross-check was added, not as a fix. I didn't open an issue, so there's no "Fixes #N".
